A [[Gender|male]] [[Teek]] '''dinghy driver''' worked as a [[ferryman]] at [[Borgo Prime|Starport Borgo]] during the [[New Republic Era]]. By [[Pilot|operating]] a [[space dinghy]] that docked with incoming [[starship]]s, it provided new arrivals with transportation to the landing platform on the [[asteroid]].

Around [[9 ABY]], the dinghy driver offered its service to the young members of the [[Skeleton Crew]]. They paid it with an [[Galactic Republic|Old Republic]] [[Galactic Credit Standard|credit]], a valuable [[currency]] it eagerly accepted before scurrying off excitedly. Later, the children planned to use the ferryman's services once again, but the deal fell through when [[Silvo's pirates|Brutus's pirate crew]] captured them. However, after their escape, the driver still ferried the younglings back to their [[Onyx Cinder|starship]].

==Biography==
===First ferrying service to the Skeleton Crew===
{{Quote|I think it wants payment.|KB, about the dinghy driver|audio=ItWantsPayment.mp3|<ref name="SC 102" />}}
[[File:DinghyDriver-WayWayOutPastTheBarrier.png|thumb|right|200px|The dinghy driver around 9 ABY]]
A [[Gender|male]] [[Teek]] worked as a [[ferryman]]<ref name="SC 102 EG" /> at [[Borgo Prime|Starport Borgo]] during the [[New Republic Era]].<ref name="SC 102" /> The driver operated a [[plastic]]-roofed [[space dinghy]] that utilized a dedicated [[Docking tube|suction tube]]<ref name="SC 102 AD">{{SkeletonCrew|Way, Way Out Past the Barrier}} {{AD|Disney+}}</ref> to dock with incoming [[starship]]s and transport new arrivals to the landing platform on the [[pirate]] [[asteroid]]. It steered the vehicle from a side-mounted [[pilot]]'s dome, holding onto a control stick.<ref name="SC 102" />

Around [[9 ABY]],<ref name="SCDate">{{SkeletonCrewDate}}</ref> the ferryman docked with the pirate starship<ref name="SC 102" /> ''[[Onyx Cinder]]''<ref name="SC 105">{{SkeletonCrew|You Have a Lot to Learn About Pirates}}</ref> and invited<ref name="SC 102" /> the [[Skeleton Crew]]<ref name="T19XifsgFS0">{{SWYouTube|T19XifsgFS0|Skeleton Crew &#124; Blast from the Past &#124; Streaming Dec 2 on Disney+}}</ref> [[youngling]]s [[Fern]], [[KB]], [[Neel]], and [[Wim]] aboard. At the suggestion of their [[droid]] [[SM-33]], the children boarded the Teek's dinghy and traveled with it to the port. After dropping off the children, the Teek jumped on Fern's back, speaking in its [[Teek language|language]] while holding out its hand. Despite not understanding its [[Language|speech]], KB figured out that the man wanted payment for its ferrying service. Therefore, Wim handed the driver an [[Galactic Republic|Old Republic]] [[Galactic Credit Standard|credit]] that his [[Wendle|father]] had given him for [[Foodstuff|lunch]] [[Currency|money]]. Since that currency was widely considered valuable,<ref name="SC 102" /> the Teek ran off excitedly<ref name="SC 102 AD" /> with the [[coin]] it had received.<ref name="SC 102" />

===The coin, the crash, and the comeback ride===
{{Quote|Wim, Wim, pay him.|Fern urges Wim to pay the dinghy driver|audio=WimPayHim.mp3|<ref name="SC 102" />}}
The dinghy driver subsequently stopped by a [[Unidentified noodle bar|noodle bar]], the same place that Neel and Wim were visiting. The man climbed onto the counter and spotted Wim paying with Old Republic credits again. The Teek jumped back down just as the [[Unidentified fry cook|fry cook]] got into a fight over the valuable coin with a [[Unidentified greasy creature|greasy patron]], onto whose shoulder the ferryman shortly scrambled in order to watch as the boys excused themselves for possessing the long-defunct currency. Wim and Neel claimed to [[Homeworld|hail]] from [[At Attin]], a mysterious [[planet]] dismissed as a myth in pirate lore. When a scuffle was about to break out, Wim up-handed his bowl of [[Unidentified noodle dish (Port Borgo)|noodles]] onto the grill to [[Fire|flame]] it up before he and Neel dashed out of the [[noodle]] bar together.<ref name="SC 102" />

[[File:GreasyAndFerryman.png|thumb|left|250px|The dinghy driver beside a greasy patron]]
Some [[time]] later, the younglings returned to the dinghy, having escaped a group of pirates<ref name="SC 102" /> and thugs<ref name="SC 102 AD" /> who had been chasing them after the skirmish in the noodle bar. Fern told Wim to pay the man again, but the communication was hindered by the ferryman speaking his non-[[Galactic Basic Standard|Basic]] language. Although the boy delved into his socket to provide the currency, the transaction was not finished, as [[Silvo's pirates|Brutus's pirate crew]] arrived for the younglings in the meantime. After [[Captain]] [[Brutus]] [[Ion rifle|incapacitated]] SM-33, the children argued with<ref name="SC 102" /> him<ref name="SC 102 AD" /> before finally being arrested pending further interrogation.<ref name="SC 102" /> While they were subsequently confined in a [[brig]], the younglings joined forces with<ref name="SC 103">{{SkeletonCrew|Very Interesting, As an Astrogation Problem}}</ref> former pirate<ref name="SC 106 AD">{{SkeletonCrew|Zero Friends Again}} {{AD|Disney+}}</ref> [[Jod Na Nawood|Silvo]] to escape and enlisted the dinghy driver's help to return to their ship. As the ferryman piloted its dinghy, the passengers discussed the state of the younglings' ship, with Silvo finding it hard to believe it was as much of a wreck as they claimed.<ref name="SC 103" /> 

==Personality and traits==
{{Listen3|Zaplata.mp3|the dinghy driver demanding payment in Teek}}
The ferryman was a [[Color|blue]]-[[eye]]d Teek with light-brown skin and a scruffy appearance, with pointed [[ear]]s and scraggly white [[hair]] covering most of its face and [[limb]]s. Small<ref name="SC 102" /> and agile,<ref name="SC 102 AD" /> it had a habit of jumping onto others' shoulders for a better view or for communication purposes.<ref name="SC 102" /> When excited, it moved hurriedly with short, quick steps.<ref name="SC 102 AD" /> It spoke the Teek language and continued using it even when faced with a communication barrier.<ref name="SC 102" />

==Equipment==
During its time piloting the dinghy, the driver wore a brown, long-sleeved [[Clothing|shirt]] with gray patches, as well as a pair of matching pants and open-toe sandals. In addition, it possessed a [[wrench]].<ref name="SC 102" />

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|I think you wanted the Teek, and then I made him look really rough because the Teeks are really fluffy.''"<br />"''Yeah, the Teeks we know from'' Battle for Endor ''is ''[sic]'' well taken care of.''"<br />"''Yeah, and this is more like a Teek that's fallen on hard times, so it was a lot skinnier and a lot of his fur has fallen out.|Jon Watts and Christopher Ford|<ref name="Jone&Weintraub:2024">{{WebCite|work=[[Wikipedia:Valnet#Collider|''Collider'']]|url=https://collider.com/star-wars-skeleton-crew-jon-watts-christopher-ford|text=Easter Eggs, Fan Theories, the Timeline – 'Star Wars: Skeleton Crew' Showrunners Answer All Your Questions|author=Jones, Tamera; Weintraub, Steven|date=2024-11-08|archivedate=20241213181732}}</ref>}}
[[File:TaxiAlien.jpg|thumb|right|200px|"Taxi Alien" by Aaron McBride, concept art of the dinghy driver]]
The driver was first mentioned in a [[StarWars.com]] article published on [[November 1]], [[2024]],<ref name="Baver:2024">{{SW|url=news/skeleton-crew-jon-watts-christopher-ford-interview|text=With ''Star Wars: Skeleton Crew'', Jon Watts and Chris Ford are Ready for Their Own Pirate Adventure}}</ref> before making its debut in "[[Way, Way Out Past the Barrier]]," the second episode of the [[Disney+]] live-action series ''[[Star Wars: Skeleton Crew]]'',<ref name="SC 102" /> which aired on [[December 2]], 2024.<ref name="Early">{{SW|url=news/skeleton-crew-premiere-date|text=''Star Wars: Skeleton Crew'' Will Debut Early|date=2024-11-26}}</ref> Based on concept art by [[Aaron McBride]],<ref name="ilmvfx:2025">{{Instagram|username=ilmvfx|url=p/DPUExx_CWWn|name=[[Industrial Light & Magic]]|date=2025-10-02|quote=Explore ILM's concept art of the Teek dinghy pilot from Star Wars: Skeleton Crew, Episode 2: "Way, Way Out Past the Barrier", now nominated for a Concept Art Award in the "Live Action Creature Art" category, and hear more about his creation below: "I did this concept art of the Teek who runs a shuttle service around the asteroid starport "Borgo Prime", ferrying visitors to and from their moored starships aboard his dinghy. I always loved Ewoks: The Battle for Endor when I was a kid. I bought it on VHS and wore out the tape from watching it so much."|archiveurl=https://archive.is/uhQJQ}}</ref> the ferryman was a handpuppet-controlled creation<ref name="Travis:2024">{{BookCite|author=Travis, Ben|container=Gravitational Pull|title=[[Wikipedia:Empire (magazine)|''Empire'']]|number=no. 434|pages=94|date=2024-10-24|publisher=0957-4948}}</ref> by [[Legacy Effects]],<ref name="legacyefx">{{WebCite|url=https://www.legacyefx.com/skeleton-crew|text=Skeleton Crew|work=[[Legacy Effects]]|archivedate=20250226013146}}</ref> digitally untouched<ref name="Travis:2024" /> except for its animated hand gestures, according to ILM Animation Supervisor [[Shawn Kelly]].<ref name="3lcxs2fnuxz2c">{{Bluesky|claytonsandell.com|post/3lcxs2fnuxz2c|[[Clayton Sandell]]|date=2024-12-10|quote=Here's a bonus example of seamlessly combining techniques for maximum storytelling effect. In this shot, the Teek ferryman is a practical puppet, except for his extended arm gesturing for payment, which is completely digital.  (I NEVER would have guessed this!) (Cont.)|archiveurl=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/oBkLJ|archivefile=File:ClaytonSandell-Teek1-Bluesky.png}}</ref><ref name="3lcxs3xhq6i27">{{Bluesky|claytonsandell.com|post/3lcxs3xhq6i27|[[Clayton Sandell]]|date=2024-12-10|quote=Shawn Kelly, ILM Animation Supervisor, on the Teek ferryman shot: "His arm is CG. So we can do some more delicate kind of gestures with his fingers and hands. He's asking to get paid. So,  he's like, 'give me some money!" (Cont.)|archiveurl=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/ExkrQ|archivefile=File:ClaytonSandell-Teek1-Bluesky.png}}</ref> Nevertheless, a single shot used a digital animation: although the puppet depicted the Teek asking for payment, a "Texas Switch" technique cut the focus to an animated model as it scurried away.<ref name="ILM">{{ILM|url=star-wars-skeleton-crew-vfx-ilm-part-1|text='Star Wars: Skeleton Crew': ILM's Visual Effects Treasure Chest, From At Attin to Starport Borgo}}</ref> For the "Way, Way Out Past the Barrier" director [[David Lowery]], seeing on screen the same, un-retouched puppet that he had previously seen on the set was a "cognitive dissonance."<ref name="Travis:2024" /> During a charity livestream with the [[501st Legion (fan organization)|501st Legion]]<ref name="j4LFqgkthT8">{{YouTube|video=j4LFqgkthT8|text=The Holiday of Hope Telethon|channel=@The501stLegion|name=[[501st Legion (fan organization)|The 501st Legion]]|date=2024-12-8}}</ref>&mdash;a fan organization of cosplay enthusiasts<ref name="501st">{{SW|url=news/the-history-of-the-501st-legion-founding-of-the-uk-garrison|text=History of the 501st Legion: Founding of the UK Garrison|nolive=1}}</ref>&mdash;actor [[Sam Witwer]] revealed that he might have voiced the character,<ref name="j4LFqgkthT8" /> which sound designer<ref name="SC 102" /> [[David W. Collins]] later confirmed.<ref name="id1072656897">{{Apple|subdomain=podcasts|url=us/podcast/episode-437-david-w-collins-returns-with-the/id1072656897?i=1000701676277|text=Episode 437 - David W. Collins RETURNS with The Sound of Skeleton Crew, Celebration Japan & more}}</ref>

[[File:DinghyLegacy.jpg|thumb|left|200px|The dinghy driver puppet by Legacy Effects]]
The dinghy driver was the first individual to be pictured within the current ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[canon]] of the Teek species, which was originally created for<ref name="SC 102 EG" /> the [[1985]] film<ref name="Vilmur:2014">{{SW|url=news/9-things-you-never-knew-about-the-ewok-adventure|text=9 Things You Never Knew About ''The Ewok Adventure''}}</ref> ''[[Ewoks: The Battle for Endor]]''.<ref name="SC 102 EG" /> The presence of a Teek character in the series was the idea of the showrunner [[Jon Watts]], while the other showrunner, [[Christopher Ford]], contributed to its portrayal by making it look "rough."<ref name="Jone&Weintraub:2024" /> As the duo first began working on the Teek, ''The Battle for Endor'' was not yet available on streaming, as it was not added to Disney+<ref name="HoloFiles">{{YouTube|video=Y1KNdWde084|channel=theholofiles|text=SKELETON CREW creators Jon Watts & Chris Ford talk new Star Wars show &#124; EXCL. ROUNDTABLE INTERVIEW|name=The HoloFiles}}</ref> until [[April 2]], [[2021]].<ref name="Ewok Disney+">{{D23|url=everything-new-you-can-stream-on-disney-in-april-2021|text=Everything New You Can Stream on Disney+ in April 2021|nolive=1}}</ref> Therefore, they received a copy of the film though an internal file sharing system.<ref name="HoloFiles" /> Accounting for the discrepancies between the ''Ewoks'' and ''Skeleton Crew'' depictions of the species, Watts compared the ferryman to a person who had "fallen on hard times," which would explain it being thinner and less hairy than other Teeks.<ref name="Jone&Weintraub:2024" />

In [[2025]], Aaron McBride's pictures of the Teek,<ref name="ilmvfx:2025" /> labeled as "Taxi Alien",<ref name="CAA 2025">{{WebCite|url=https://www.conceptartassociation.com/caa-2025|text=CAA 2025|work=Concept Art Association|archivedate=20251030224453}}</ref> were nominated for a Concept Art Award in the "Live Action Creature Art" category. On that occasion, McBride mentioned watching ''Ewoks: The Battle for Endor'' on a VHS tape in his childhood.<ref name="ilmvfx:2025" /> Although they made it to the final, the pictures of Teek did not win in their category, losing to McBride's concept art of "blind bats" from [[wikipedia:The Lord of the Rings: The Rings of Power season 2|''The Lord of the Rings: The Rings of Power'' Season 2]].<ref name="CAA 2025" />

